1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,353
Both statements are true. The scanner's
finding is real, and so is the engineer's

2
00:00:09,393 --> 00:00:14,426
protest. The source code of the notebook
reads the key from an environment variable,

3
00:00:14,466 --> 00:00:18,957
which is the right way to handle a key.
Nobody typed the key into any file.

4
00:00:19,297 --> 00:00:23,861
The key was printed by a cell, and the
notebook file stores what cells print.

5
00:00:23,901 --> 00:00:28,884
That one fact also explains why a notebook
that nobody edited shows as modified, and

6
00:00:28,924 --> 00:00:33,787
why two people who reran the same notebook
get a merge conflict that leaves a file

7
00:00:33,827 --> 00:00:37,674
Jupyter can't open. Keep the
notebook that nobody edited in mind.

8
00:00:37,714 --> 00:00:40,066
You'll count its changed lines yourself.

9
00:00:41,906 --> 00:00:45,878
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

10
00:00:45,918 --> 00:00:50,548
This is the first of four videos on
AI and ML repositories, Chapter 28.

11
00:00:50,588 --> 00:00:56,072
Three words first. A commit is one saved
snapshot of the project, and history is the

12
00:00:56,112 --> 00:00:57,454
chain of all of them.

13
00:00:57,794 --> 00:01:02,639
A notebook is a document that mixes cells
of code with the results of running them.

14
00:01:02,679 --> 00:01:07,524
And Jupyter is the program that runs it.
The rule that organizes the chapter is one

15
00:01:07,564 --> 00:01:12,350
sentence: keep code and pointers in Git,
and keep data, weights and outputs out of

16
00:01:12,390 --> 00:01:12,527
it.

17
00:01:12,867 --> 00:01:17,489
An ML project differs from a backend service
in that most of its bytes aren't source

18
00:01:17,529 --> 00:01:21,763
code, and most of what determines a
result isn't in a commit unless you put a

19
00:01:21,803 --> 00:01:22,984
reference to it there.

20
00:01:23,324 --> 00:01:27,627
Two limits of the chapter, stated
once, as the textbook states them.

21
00:01:27,667 --> 00:01:32,863
The tools of this field, nbstripout, nbdime,
jupytext, DVC, pre-commit and others,

22
00:01:32,903 --> 00:01:38,099
aren't installed in the lab. Their commands
are given from their documentation, in

23
00:01:38,139 --> 00:01:43,271
blocks without output, with the versions
the course's research report recorded on

24
00:01:43,311 --> 00:01:48,635
the first of October 2026. What's run here
is the mechanism under each tool, written

25
00:01:48,675 --> 00:01:50,742
with the Python standard library.

26
00:01:51,082 --> 00:01:55,495
The filter you'll see in the terminal is
a small stand-in written for the lab.

27
00:01:55,535 --> 00:01:57,321
It isn't one of the named tools.

28
00:01:57,661 --> 00:02:02,573
From video 88 you know what a clean filter
is: a command that rewrites a file's

29
00:02:02,613 --> 00:02:07,587
content on its way into the repository.
.gitattributes names a driver for a path

30
00:02:07,627 --> 00:02:11,035
pattern, and the Git configuration
defines the command.

31
00:02:11,075 --> 00:02:14,795
Today's video takes that in the
directions an ML team needs.

32
00:02:15,135 --> 00:02:20,003
The demos use docqa, a small project that
classifies support tickets and answers

33
00:02:20,043 --> 00:02:21,476
them from documentation.

34
00:02:23,316 --> 00:02:24,894
Here's what you'll walk away with.

35
00:02:25,234 --> 00:02:26,646
After this video you can:

36
00:02:26,986 --> 00:02:31,577
Decide for a file in an ML project whether
it belongs in Git, is referenced from

37
00:02:31,617 --> 00:02:32,677
Git, or is ignored.

38
00:02:33,017 --> 00:02:37,609
Explain what a notebook file stores and
why a rerun changes it without any edit.

39
00:02:37,949 --> 00:02:41,527
Show how a notebook leaks a secret
or data through its outputs.

40
00:02:41,867 --> 00:02:46,898
Install a clean filter that strips outputs,
mark it required, and verify it in CI.

41
00:02:47,238 --> 00:02:52,534
Say what nbdime, jupytext and ReviewNB add
to the filter, as the section describes

42
00:02:52,574 --> 00:02:52,859
them.

43
00:02:54,699 --> 00:02:59,431
What belongs in Git. Git should hold what
a human writes and reviews, plus small

44
00:02:59,471 --> 00:03:04,382
references to everything else. It shouldn't
hold what a program produces or what is

45
00:03:04,422 --> 00:03:04,740
large.

46
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:10,233
Three properties of Git decide the question.
Every clone carries the full history,

47
00:03:10,273 --> 00:03:15,552
so a file committed once is downloaded by
everyone, in every version, for as long as

48
00:03:15,592 --> 00:03:20,618
the history exists. Objects can't be
recalled: removing a file adds a commit and

49
00:03:20,658 --> 00:03:24,733
removes nothing. And diff, merge
and blame work on lines of text.

50
00:03:25,073 --> 00:03:30,264
On top of that the platform sets limits:
GitHub warns about files larger than 50

51
00:03:30,304 --> 00:03:35,755
mebibytes, blocks files larger than 100
mebibytes, recommends that repositories stay

52
00:03:35,795 --> 00:03:41,116
ideally under 1 gigabyte and strongly under
5 gigabytes, and states that Git isn't

53
00:03:41,156 --> 00:03:43,404
designed to serve as a backup tool.

54
00:03:43,744 --> 00:03:47,937
Read it as two columns: what Git
holds, and what Git only references.

55
00:03:48,277 --> 00:03:53,988
Quick quiz. Three files: a prompt for the
model, a checkpoint of trained weights,

56
00:03:54,028 --> 00:03:58,178
and a lock file. Which one stays
out of Git? A, the prompt.

57
00:03:58,218 --> 00:04:01,658
B, the checkpoint. C, the
lock file. Your answer?

58
00:04:06,498 --> 00:04:11,648
B, the checkpoint. It's large, a program
produced it, and Git holds a pointer to it

59
00:04:11,688 --> 00:04:15,901
instead. The prompt and the lock
file were on the left of the table.

60
00:04:16,241 --> 00:04:21,059
The textbook is careful about the status
of this table: the split is the research

61
00:04:21,099 --> 00:04:24,717
report's inference from the
limits, not a published standard.

62
00:04:24,757 --> 00:04:29,635
The less obvious rows are prompts and
pointers. A prompt is source code for an LLM

63
00:04:29,675 --> 00:04:34,613
application: a one-word change alters
behavior, so it deserves a diff, a review and

64
00:04:34,653 --> 00:04:39,651
a commit. A pointer is what makes the
right-hand column reproducible: Git can't hold

65
00:04:39,691 --> 00:04:44,569
the data, but it can hold forty or sixty-four
hexadecimal characters that identify

66
00:04:44,609 --> 00:04:46,129
exactly one version of it.

67
00:04:46,469 --> 00:04:51,501
The same split is a security boundary. The
left column is what you're prepared to

68
00:04:51,541 --> 00:04:55,258
show every engineer, contractor
and CI job with read access.

69
00:04:55,598 --> 00:05:00,863
Notebooks. An .ipynb file is one JSON
document in which each code cell carries its

70
00:05:00,903 --> 00:05:06,297
source, the outputs of its last run and a
counter, so running a notebook changes the

71
00:05:06,337 --> 00:05:08,302
file even when no code changed.

72
00:05:08,642 --> 00:05:13,882
JSON is a plain-text format of names and
values. Precisely: the notebook format

73
00:05:13,922 --> 00:05:19,027
defines a top-level object with metadata,
nbformat, nbformat_minor and cells.

74
00:05:19,067 --> 00:05:23,572
A code cell stores source, a list
of outputs and an execution_count.

75
00:05:23,612 --> 00:05:28,985
An output can be a stream, which is what
was printed, a result, an error with its

76
00:05:29,025 --> 00:05:32,861
traceback, or display data such
as a base64-encoded image.

77
00:05:33,201 --> 00:05:38,060
That one design decision causes three
separate problems, and the demo shows each: a

78
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:42,015
rerun is a change. Outputs carry
data and credentials into history.

79
00:05:42,055 --> 00:05:45,793
And a line-based merge can produce
a file that isn't a notebook.

80
00:05:46,133 --> 00:05:50,871
A clean filter that strips outputs. A clean
filter rewrites a notebook on its way

81
00:05:50,911 --> 00:05:55,767
into the repository so that the blob has
no outputs, while the file in your working

82
00:05:55,807 --> 00:06:00,309
tree keeps them. The blob is Git's stored
copy of the file's content, and the

83
00:06:00,349 --> 00:06:03,494
working tree is the folder
of ordinary files you edit.

84
00:06:03,834 --> 00:06:10,181
The maintained implementation is
nbstripout, version 0.9.1 of February 2026.

85
00:06:10,221 --> 00:06:13,290
From its documentation, not run here:

86
00:06:13,630 --> 00:06:18,651
Its README states the limitation that shapes
everything else: there's no way to have

87
00:06:18,691 --> 00:06:23,652
Git set up the filter automatically when
someone clones a repository, by design, so

88
00:06:23,692 --> 00:06:26,243
that a clone never
executes arbitrary code.

89
00:06:26,583 --> 00:06:29,861
What required changes. A
filter is optional by default.

90
00:06:29,901 --> 00:06:34,807
The manual says a missing driver definition,
or a driver that exits with an error,

91
00:06:34,847 --> 00:06:38,185
"is not an error but makes
the filter a no-op passthru".

92
00:06:38,225 --> 00:06:43,192
For a filter whose purpose is to keep outputs
out of history, a silent pass-through

93
00:06:43,232 --> 00:06:45,002
is the worst possible failure.

94
00:06:45,342 --> 00:06:49,312
The complementary tools. They're
complementary, not alternatives.

95
00:06:49,652 --> 00:06:54,648
nbdime understands the notebook structure,
so it can show a cell-level diff and can

96
00:06:54,688 --> 00:06:59,625
merge two reruns by resolving generated
values such as execution counters, instead

97
00:06:59,665 --> 00:07:04,540
of writing conflict markers into JSON.
Like every driver, its definitions live in

98
00:07:04,580 --> 00:07:09,577
each clone's configuration: a colleague who
hasn't run its setup command gets Git's

99
00:07:09,617 --> 00:07:11,822
line-based behavior, with no warning.

100
00:07:12,162 --> 00:07:16,064
jupytext takes the other road:
don't review the .ipynb at all.

101
00:07:16,104 --> 00:07:21,406
It pairs the notebook with a text file that
contains only the cell inputs, so a pull

102
00:07:21,446 --> 00:07:26,747
request shows an ordinary Python diff. The
README's own recipe is to commit the text

103
00:07:26,787 --> 00:07:31,707
file and, unless you want outputs versioned,
to keep the .ipynb out of version

104
00:07:31,747 --> 00:07:32,215
control.

105
00:07:32,555 --> 00:07:38,495
The last row is the durable fix. A function
in src/docqa/ has tests, a diff and a

106
00:07:38,535 --> 00:07:41,375
blame history. A cell has none of them.

107
00:07:41,715 --> 00:07:45,506
GitHub, not Git. GitHub renders
.ipynb files as static HTML.

108
00:07:45,546 --> 00:07:50,805
Its rich notebook diff is still the feature
preview announced on the first of March

109
00:07:50,845 --> 00:07:56,105
2023, in which you can't comment on lines.
No general-availability announcement was

110
00:07:56,145 --> 00:08:01,469
found. Unverified: whether the redesigned
"Files changed" page renders notebook rich

111
00:08:01,509 --> 00:08:04,853
diffs. The report didn't
check a signed-in interface.

112
00:08:04,893 --> 00:08:08,620
Treat it as a viewer, not as
the basis of a review process.

113
00:08:10,460 --> 00:08:13,902
Two analogies from the textbook,
one per half of the video.

114
00:08:14,242 --> 00:08:17,403
For what belongs in Git: a
lab notebook and a freezer.

115
00:08:17,443 --> 00:08:22,027
The notebook records the protocol, the
settings and the label of every sample.

116
00:08:22,067 --> 00:08:27,006
The samples live in the freezer. Nobody
tapes a sample into the notebook, and nobody

117
00:08:27,046 --> 00:08:31,630
runs a lab from the freezer alone. The
analogy breaks in one useful way: a Git

118
00:08:31,670 --> 00:08:36,610
"notebook" can verify the freezer, because
the label it records can be a checksum of

119
00:08:36,650 --> 00:08:37,380
the contents.

120
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:42,727
For the filter: a mail room that removes
attachments from outgoing letters and files

121
00:08:42,767 --> 00:08:46,272
the plain letter. Your desk
copy still has the attachments.

122
00:08:46,312 --> 00:08:51,079
Where it breaks: every desk has its own
mail room, and a new employee's desk has

123
00:08:51,119 --> 00:08:52,942
none until somebody sets it up.

124
00:08:53,282 --> 00:08:56,598
That last sentence is the whole
second half of the video.

125
00:08:56,638 --> 00:09:00,485
The attribute travels with the
repository. The mail room does not.

126
00:09:00,525 --> 00:09:04,371
So the filter is a convenience,
and the control is something else.

127
00:09:04,711 --> 00:09:09,862
Try it now, on paper, for thirty seconds.
Write down the three things a code cell

128
00:09:09,902 --> 00:09:13,259
stores, and put a tick beside
the one a human writes.

129
00:09:13,299 --> 00:09:13,900
I'll wait.

130
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:24,704
One object, three kinds of content: a
counter, the outputs and the source.

131
00:09:24,744 --> 00:09:28,112
A human wrote one of them,
the source. That's your tick.

132
00:09:28,452 --> 00:09:33,788
And the root-cause box of section 28.3.
Read the root cause line: one file mixes

133
00:09:33,828 --> 00:09:37,014
what a human wrote with
what a program produced.

134
00:09:38,854 --> 00:09:44,090
Into the lab. It can't run Jupyter, so a
script writes the file Jupyter would save

135
00:09:44,130 --> 00:09:47,950
after each run. The key in it
is a fake one, and it says so.

136
00:09:48,290 --> 00:09:50,641
Step 1: the anatomy of one cell.

137
00:09:53,981 --> 00:09:58,856
Source, output and counter sit in one object.
Read the source: it takes the key from

138
00:09:58,896 --> 00:10:03,244
the environment and prints it. Read the
output: the printed line is stored.

139
00:10:03,584 --> 00:10:08,474
Step 2: a rerun is a change. Run all
cells again without editing anything.

140
00:10:08,514 --> 00:10:13,538
Predict: how many lines will git diff
report, and how many of them are code?

141
00:10:13,578 --> 00:10:14,604
Say it out loud.

142
00:10:19,444 --> 00:10:24,150
Six lines changed and none of them is
code: three counters, one of them stored

143
00:10:24,190 --> 00:10:29,079
twice, one printed line, one image. There's
the notebook that nobody edited, from

144
00:10:29,119 --> 00:10:33,946
the opening. A reviewer who opens this
diff learns nothing, and after a few such

145
00:10:33,986 --> 00:10:36,563
commits nobody opens
notebook diffs at all.

146
00:10:36,903 --> 00:10:40,306
Step 3: outputs carry
credentials into history.

147
00:10:40,646 --> 00:10:45,968
The value is in two commits. And 2,429 of
3,558 bytes of this small notebook are

148
00:10:46,008 --> 00:10:49,655
outputs. With real charts the
proportion is far higher.

149
00:10:49,695 --> 00:10:55,218
The same holds for a data frame preview
that shows customer rows: no secret scanner

150
00:10:55,258 --> 00:10:58,703
has a pattern for "a table
that should not be here".

151
00:10:58,743 --> 00:11:04,333
One caveat the textbook marks as unverified:
how often secrets leak through notebook

152
00:11:04,373 --> 00:11:08,690
outputs specifically. The research
found mechanisms, not numbers.

153
00:11:09,030 --> 00:11:13,509
Step 4: the merge. Two people rerun
the same notebook on two branches.

154
00:11:13,549 --> 00:11:17,576
🟡 CAUTION: git merge moves the
current branch when it succeeds.

155
00:11:17,616 --> 00:11:21,062
Predict: will the file be a
valid notebook afterwards?

156
00:11:21,102 --> 00:11:21,707
Yes or no?

157
00:11:26,547 --> 00:11:30,713
Six conflict regions, written into
the middle of a JSON document.

158
00:11:30,753 --> 00:11:35,954
The file no longer parses. Jupyter can't
open it to let you resolve the conflict.

159
00:11:35,994 --> 00:11:38,866
The root-cause box from
the diagram says why.

160
00:11:39,206 --> 00:11:41,638
Step 5: how such a tool works.

161
00:11:41,978 --> 00:11:46,976
Parse the JSON, empty outputs, set
execution_count to null, drop the metadata that

162
00:11:47,016 --> 00:11:51,767
execution adds, and write the document
back in the layout Jupyter itself uses.

163
00:11:51,807 --> 00:11:56,927
The last part matters: if the filter
reformatted the file, every notebook would show

164
00:11:56,967 --> 00:12:01,903
as changed the first time it passed through.
Cleaning cleaned content must change

165
00:12:01,943 --> 00:12:02,395
nothing.

166
00:12:05,735 --> 00:12:12,910
Step 6: configure. 🟡 CAUTION: git config
set changes .git/config, a file that keeps

167
00:12:12,950 --> 00:12:17,778
no history. The subcommand
form needs Git 2.46 or later.

168
00:12:18,118 --> 00:12:21,133
The attribute is already
committed in .gitattributes.

169
00:12:21,173 --> 00:12:24,418
git check-attr confirms which
driver applies to the path.

170
00:12:24,458 --> 00:12:27,819
The driver itself is defined
in this clone's configuration.

171
00:12:28,159 --> 00:12:33,667
Step 7: add and commit. Predict: after the
commit, how many outputs does the file on

172
00:12:33,707 --> 00:12:37,695
disk have, and how many does the
blob have? Say both numbers.

173
00:12:42,535 --> 00:12:46,844
The file on disk has three
outputs and 3,558 bytes.

174
00:12:46,884 --> 00:12:53,836
The blob has none and 967 bytes. The checks:
line is the project's pre-commit hook

175
00:12:53,876 --> 00:12:56,309
reporting. That is video 177.

176
00:12:59,649 --> 00:13:04,888
The first row: configuring the filter
only adds the driver to .git/config.

177
00:13:05,228 --> 00:13:09,926
The second row: git add leaves the outputs
in the working tree, and the index entry

178
00:13:09,966 --> 00:13:12,210
points at a blob of the cleaned content.

179
00:13:12,550 --> 00:13:16,780
The third row is how you apply a new filter
to notebooks that are already tracked.

180
00:13:17,120 --> 00:13:21,703
The last row is the price: the outputs
exist only in your working tree, and any

181
00:13:21,743 --> 00:13:25,507
command that rewrites the file
from the repository discards them.

182
00:13:25,847 --> 00:13:28,695
Step 8: rerunning is no longer a change.

183
00:13:29,035 --> 00:13:33,349
The file contains the second run's
printed key, and git status is silent.

184
00:13:33,689 --> 00:13:38,679
Why silent? Git compares the cleaned form
of the file with the index, its list of

185
00:13:38,719 --> 00:13:43,708
what the next commit will hold. Cleaned,
the first run and the rerun are the same

186
00:13:43,748 --> 00:13:47,061
bytes, and the same bytes
always get the same blob ID.

187
00:13:47,401 --> 00:13:52,111
A change to the code of a cell still shows,
as a one-line diff a reviewer can read:

188
00:13:52,451 --> 00:13:57,392
Step 9: what required changes. The driver
is pointed at a program that doesn't

189
00:13:57,432 --> 00:14:02,437
exist. Predict the exit status of git add
with required set, and with it unset.

190
00:14:07,277 --> 00:14:12,346
With required = true, git add fails
with status 128 and nothing is staged.

191
00:14:12,386 --> 00:14:17,800
With it unset, the same three error lines
appear, the exit status is 0, and the

192
00:14:17,840 --> 00:14:20,699
notebook is staged with its three outputs.

193
00:14:21,039 --> 00:14:24,287
nbstripout declares its filter
required for this reason.

194
00:14:24,627 --> 00:14:29,782
Step 10: what a clone has. Predict: Asha
clones. Which of the two halves does she

195
00:14:29,822 --> 00:14:31,899
get, the attribute or the driver?

196
00:14:36,739 --> 00:14:40,667
The attribute travelled, because
.gitattributes is a tracked file.

197
00:14:41,007 --> 00:14:45,578
The driver did not, because it lives
in .git/config, and Git never copies

198
00:14:45,618 --> 00:14:50,631
configuration from a clone source: if it
did, cloning a repository would let its

199
00:14:50,671 --> 00:14:55,873
author run commands on your machine. required
= true didn't help either, since that

200
00:14:55,913 --> 00:15:00,863
setting is part of the same missing
configuration. Asha's notebook went in with

201
00:15:00,903 --> 00:15:04,273
outputs and nothing warned
her. She did nothing wrong.

202
00:15:04,313 --> 00:15:05,852
Her clone was never told.

203
00:15:06,192 --> 00:15:08,618
Step 11: the check that does travel.

204
00:15:08,958 --> 00:15:13,932
--verify strips each committed notebook
in memory and fails if the result differs

205
00:15:13,972 --> 00:15:18,946
from the file. On a fresh checkout the
files are the blobs, so this is a check of

206
00:15:18,986 --> 00:15:23,589
what is in history. It fails on Asha's
commit and passes on the one before.

207
00:15:23,929 --> 00:15:28,911
Run as a required check in CI, the automation
that tests every change on a server,

208
00:15:28,951 --> 00:15:33,995
it's the control. The filter on each laptop
is a convenience that keeps people from

209
00:15:34,035 --> 00:15:34,730
tripping it.

210
00:15:36,570 --> 00:15:38,218
Five mistakes to watch for.

211
00:15:38,558 --> 00:15:41,893
Believing "we use nbstripout"
